-- title:
--   Integrality over ℤ[j(q)] gives integrality over ℤ((q))
-- statement:
--   Write $\mathrm{coeffMap}$ for the ring homomorphism $\mathbb{Z}((q)) \to \mathbb{Q}((q))$ on formal Laurent series (`HahnSeries ℤ`-type series) induced coefficientwise by the canonical map $\mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{Q}$, and let [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157) be the element of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ given by $q^{-1}$ (the Hahn series $\mathrm{single}(-1,1)$) times the Laurent series attached to the power series [`ModularCurve.jNumQ`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L149), itself the coefficientwise image in $\mathbb{Q}[[q]]$ of the integral power series [`ModularCurve.jNum`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L142). Let $y \in \mathbb{Q}((q))$ and assume that $y$ is integral over the $\mathbb{Z}$-subalgebra $\mathbb{Z}[\mathrm{jq}]$ of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ generated by [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157), that is, $y$ satisfies a monic polynomial with coefficients in that subalgebra. The conclusion is that $y$ is an integral element for $\mathrm{coeffMap}$ in the sense of `RingHom.IsIntegralElem`: there exists a monic polynomial $P$ with coefficients in $\mathbb{Z}((q))$ such that evaluating $P$ at $y$ along $\mathrm{coeffMap}$ gives $0$; equivalently, $y$ is integral over the image of $\mathbb{Z}((q))$ in $\mathbb{Q}((q))$.
--
--   This is the step that replaces integrality over the small ring $\mathbb{Z}[j(q)]$ by integrality over the coefficientwise image of $\mathbb{Z}((q))$, the point being that the $q$-expansion of $j$ has integral coefficients so that $\mathbb{Z}[\mathrm{jq}] \subseteq \mathbb{Z}((q))$. It feeds the bounded-denominators argument, where integral closedness of $\mathbb{Z}((q))$ then forces integral coefficients, and is used by [`ModularCurve.exists_ne_zero_coeffMap_eq_mul_of_isModPFormFn`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_ne_zero_coeffMap_eq_mul_of_isModPFormFn).
-- source:
